# studio-sync log files are written LF on both machines; keep them byte-identical (no CRLF on Windows)
server/studio-data/*.jsonl text eol=lf
# server/lexicon/*.json is bind-mounted into the running container AND
# edited from a Linux mount (Claude's device-bridge sandbox writes plain
# LF), while lexicon-watch.sh runs through Git Bash on fieldy's Windows
# box. Without this, right after lexicon-sync.sh commits, Windows git's
# own line-ending normalization sees the just-committed LF content as
# "should be CRLF" and re-marks the file dirty in the working tree --
# so the very next `git pull --rebase` in the same script run aborts
# with "You have unstaged changes. Please commit or stash them.",
# silently blocking the push step (no error surfaced beyond the log).
# Six lexicon+translation commits piled up unpushed for ~2 hours before
# this was traced. Forcing LF for these files means git never considers
# a fresh checkout "modified" on either machine, so the phantom dirty
# state can't happen. Fieldy, 2026-09-15, after "syriac seemed to have
# gotten skipped" turned out to be every recent lexicon commit stuck
# unpushed for the same CRLF-churn reason.
server/lexicon/*.json text eol=lf
